Q: Getting random number using OpenSSL A: <p>Just use <a href="https://docs.openssl.org/3.0/man3/RAND_bytes/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>RAND_bytes</code></a>, it's pretty straightforward.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cstdio&gt;
#include &lt;iomanip&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;openssl/err.h&gt;
#include &lt;openssl/rand.h&gt;

int main() {
    std::vector&lt;unsigned char&gt; buf(8);
    int ret = RAND_bytes(buf.data(), buf.size());
    if (ret != 1) {
        std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;RAND_bytes failed!&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
        ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
        return 1;
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::hex &lt;&lt; std::setw(2) &lt;&lt; std::setfill('0');
    for (const auto&amp; c : buf) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; (unsigned) c;
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::endl;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Do not use <code>RAND_seed</code>. It allows you to seed the random generator manually, but that's not something you do in a normal application. By default, the random generator is safely seeded from the operating system.</p>
